{
  "dom_id": "step-e10879",
  "name": "Example: Identifying True Statements Regarding Product and Quotient Properties",
  "problem": "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $z^7 = 1$\n\n- $(z+z)^7 = 1$\n\n- $z+z=2z$ is a $7$th root of unity",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $z$ is a $7$th root of unity, then (by definition), we have that \n\n$$\nz^7=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+z)^{7} & =(2z)^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{7}\\cdot {z}^{7} \\\\  & =128\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =128 \\\\  & \\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement II, we have that $(z+z)^7 \\neq 1,$ which means that $z+z=2z$ is *not* a $7$th root of unity. \n\nIn general, the sum of two $n$th roots of unity is *not* an $n$th root of unity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
